<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium&quot; is a medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hymn attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> (1225–1274), written for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Feast of Corpus Christi</a>. It is also sung on Maundy Thursday, during the procession that carries the Blessed Sacrament from the church to the place where it is kept until Good Friday.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The opening words recall an older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hymn from which this one is derived, the Pange lingua gloriosi proelium certaminis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venantius-fortunatus">Venantius Fortunatus</a>. Two plainchant settings survive. The better known is a tune in the Phrygian mode, Mode III, from the Roman liturgy, which was originally part of the Gallican Rite; the other comes from the Mozarabic liturgy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The hymn expresses the doctrine that the bread and wine are changed into the body and blood of Christ during Mass. It tells of the mystery of the body and the precious blood shed for the world's redemption, of Christ born of a virgin, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a>, at which he gives himself as food to the Twelve with his own hands; where the senses fail, faith alone suffices.</p><p>Its last two stanzas, beginning Tantum ergo, are sung at Benediction of the Blessed Sacrament and close with a doxology to the Father, the Son and the Spirit. There are many English translations, among them one by Edward Caswall, and in English it is often sung to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> tune under the title Of the Glorious Body Telling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin des Prez</a> based his Missa Pange lingua, one of his last works, dated to between 1515 and 1521, on the Roman tune, and many commentators count it among the high points of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> polyphony. Juan de Urrede, a Flemish composer active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the late 15th century, set the hymn many times, mostly on the Mozarabic melody. The theme of its third line in Josquin's Kyrie was later used by composers from Johann Jakob Froberger to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Anton Bruckner</a>'s first composition was a setting of the first strophe.</p><p>The Tantum ergo has been set separately by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pierluigi-da-palestrina">Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-schubert">Franz Schubert</a>, Maurice Duruflé and others. An organ reharmonisation of the hymn by Beethoven was found in October 2012 by the musicologist Barry Cooper. In 1818 Pius VII granted an indulgence of 300 days for each recitation of the whole hymn and 100 days for the Tantum ergo. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> translation was used at benediction in the Syro-Malabar Catholic Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> until the 1970s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</b>&quot; is a medieval Latin hymn attributed to Thomas Aquinas (1225–1274), written for the Feast of Corpus Christi.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a></li></ul></section>
